.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell
.Nd apply a migration repository, unattended
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -repo Ar directory
.Op Fl -repo Ar directory ...
.Op Fl -status | Fl -dry-run | Fl -dry-run=chain
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Ar conninfo
.Nm
.Op Fl V | Fl -version
.Op Fl h | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
applies every migration a repository has pending, in dependency order, and
exits with a code.
It is the deployment half of a pair:
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
is for
.Em authoring
a migration, where an agent plans one, reads the warnings and decides;
.Nm
is for
.Em applying
one on a release pipeline, where there is nobody to ask.
.Pp
.Sy Nothing about the decisions needs an agent ,
which is the part worth stating because the opposite is the natural assumption.
Pacing is measured rather than decided: the executor commits on a lock waiter,
an interval or a row cap, and the observer throttles on the transitive waiter
count and cancels a batch that starves one.
Ordering is declared rather than decided:
.Va depends_on
is a field of the signed specification.
And the concurrency grouping a repository reports is already the conservative
one, because it denies concurrency both on a shared relation, which is
decidable, and on incomplete evidence, which is the case that matters -- an
opaque trigger function can write any table, so a migration whose relation list
cannot be proven complete is never grouped with another.
.Pp
.Nm
therefore honours what the repository derived and never widens it: it does not
reorder a level, does not merge two groups, and does not run an
.Ql undecided
pair together.
Advisory locks refuse an unsafe overlap whatever anybody decided.
.Ss Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -repo Ar directory
The directory of signed specifications.
Required; there is nothing to apply without one.
.Pp
Repeatable.
Several directories are applied as ONE repository: the union is ordered as a
single graph, so
.Cm depends_on
crosses freely between projects, and a specification id claimed by two of them
is refused rather than resolved.
This works because the ledger records a specification's id, digest and bytes and
never a file path, so which directory a migration was read from changes nothing
about whether it has been applied.
The order the directories are named in does not affect the listing.
.It Fl -manifest Ar file
A manifest naming the sources this deployment is made of, as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "laswell_manifest_version": 1,
  "complete": true,
  "sources": [ { "name": "shop",  "directory": "db/shop" },
               { "name": "audit", "directory": "db/audit" } ] }
.Ed
.Pp
Directories are resolved against the manifest's own folder, so a manifest
checked out anywhere names the same sources.
.Pp
.Cm complete
is the part that matters and is an assertion no directory can make about
itself: it says these sources are ALL of this database's story.
An epoch lets a partial deployment answer only for the lineages it brought;
a complete manifest says there is nothing else to answer for, which puts the
drift check back to full strength \(em every migration the ledger records must
be described by a file here, in any epoch.
It only ever widens the check, so omitting it costs scope and never safety.
.It Fl -status
Report what is pending, held, or for another environment, and exit.
Changes nothing.
.It Fl -dry-run
Plan every pending migration and apply none.
Each plan is tried against the real schema in a transaction that is rolled
back, so a refusal here is a refusal that would have happened.
Each is planned against the database as it is
.Em now ,
so in a repository not yet applied, one that needs what an earlier pending
migration creates is refused; use
.Fl -dry-run=chain
for that.
.It Fl -dry-run=chain
Rehearse every pending migration in dependency order, each planned and applied
on top of the ones before it, then roll all of it back.
A migration that fails is reported and the chain continues; what depends on it
is reported as not rehearsed.
It holds every lock until the end, so it is for an empty database or a restored
copy, not production.
See the
.Em DRY RUN
section of
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 .
.It Fl c Ar file , Fl -config Ar file
Configuration file.
Also read from
.Ev PGLASWELL_CONFIG .
.It Ar conninfo
A libpq connection string.
Also read from
.Ev DATABASE_URL .
.El
.Ss Modules
A binary built with a vendor module says so:
.Ql pg_laswell --version
prints
.Ql modules: citus
after the version.
Such a binary also applies that vendor's kinds, and reads the module's
settings from a connection's section of the configuration file, such as
.Va citus.workers ,
the hosts a Citus cluster's workers are.
Settings describe the target rather than the change, so one repository can
be applied to a plain PostgreSQL and to a Citus cluster, each with its own
configuration file; see
.Xr pg_laswell_citus 7 .
A binary without the module refuses a specification naming one of its kinds,
and refuses a configuration naming one of its settings.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Gate a build on whether anything is pending or has drifted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell --repo ./migrations --status || exit 1
.Ed
.Pp
Plan everything without applying it, on the real schema:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell --repo ./migrations --dry-run
.Ed
.Pp
Apply, in a deployment step:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell --repo ./migrations
.Ed
.Pp
Apply one repository to a developer's PostgreSQL and a Citus staging cluster,
each configuration naming its own connection and, for Citus, its workers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pg_laswell --config dev.ini     --repo ./migrations
pg_laswell --config staging.ini --repo ./migrations
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_citus 7
.Pp
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
is the authoritative reference for the specification format, pacing, trust,
bootstrap and the ledger.
This page documents only what differs when there is no agent.
